<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morbihan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulf of Morbihan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Morbihan is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Brittany in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, with its prefecture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>, named after the Gulf of Morbihan, Breton for 'little sea'. The gulf is an inland sea dotted with islands, opening to the Atlantic through a narrow channel. The department is famous for its prehistoric monuments, including the stone alignments of Carnac, and was the land of the Gallic Veneti.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morbihan.jpg" alt="Morbihan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fab5669 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vannes_-_pr%C3%A9fecture_(1).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Morbihan is one of the original 83 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> created on 4 March 1790 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. It was formed from part of the Province of Brittany and takes its name from the Morbihan, the enclosed 'small sea' in Breton that is the principal feature of its coastline. The Carnac stones, the megalithic alignments of Carnac, stand in the department and are noted as older and more extensive than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stonehenge">Stonehenge</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>.</p><p>In 1945 cadets of the École Spéciale Militaire de Saint-Cyr, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s foremost military academy for officers, were relocated to Camp Coëtquidan at Guer. The camp was later developed to include the École militaire interarmes, for non-commissioned officers, and the École Militaire du Corps Technique et Administratif, so that three major military educational facilities now stand at Guer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> lies in the northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and is bounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finistere">Finistère</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotes-darmor">Côtes-d'Armor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ille-et-vilaine">Ille-et-Vilaine</a> and Loire-Atlantique, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> to the southwest. The Gulf of Morbihan holds many islands, 365 according to legend, though in fact there are between 30 and 40 depending on how they are counted, as well as islets too small for any development. All but two of them, l'Île-aux-Moines and l'Île-d'Arz, are privately owned.</p><p>Outside the gulf the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> has four further inhabited islands: Belle Île, Groix, Houat and Hoëdic. Meaban, just outside the Port du Crouesty, is an ornithological reserve that visitors may not enter. The most populous commune is Lorient, and the prefecture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>, is the second.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Many residents support the maintenance and use of the Breton language, and there are numerous advocates of bilingual education. The painter Raymond Wintz (1884–1956) depicted places around the Gulf of Morbihan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Morbihan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Veneti held the coast of the <b>Morbihan</b>, a landscape of tidal inlets and promontories, with Darioritum (Vannes) as their later chief town.</blockquote><a href="/state/veneti">Veneti</a></li></ul></section>
